WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.501 --> 00:00:03.467
-Our first guest tonight is an Emmy-, Golden Globe-,

00:00:03.467 --> 00:00:05.667
and Tony-winning actor you know from the show "Taxi"

00:00:05.667 --> 00:00:07.167
and films like "Ordinary People,"

00:00:07.167 --> 00:00:08.834
"Independence Day," and "Uncut Gems."

00:00:08.834 --> 00:00:10.100
He received an Oscar nomination

00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:11.801
for Best Supporting Actor for his work

00:00:11.801 --> 00:00:14.400
in "The Fabelmans," which is in theaters and on demand now.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:15.934
Let's take a look.

00:00:15.934 --> 00:00:17.767
-Family, we love with art.

00:00:17.767 --> 00:00:20.601
We meshuga for art.

00:00:20.601 --> 00:00:23.133
You think I wanted to leave my sisters, my mama, and my papa

00:00:23.133 --> 00:00:27.200
and go stick my stupid head in the mouth of lions?

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:29.400
-P-Putting your head in a lion's mouth is art?

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:33.167
-[ Laughs ] No.

00:00:33.167 --> 00:00:37.133
Sticking your head in the mouth of lions was balls.

00:00:37.133 --> 00:00:41.200
Making sure the lion don't eat my head, that is art.

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:44.100
-Please welcome to the show Judd Hirsch, everybody.

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:45.667
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:45.667 --> 00:00:52.834
♪♪

00:00:52.834 --> 00:01:00.100
♪♪

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:07.267
♪♪

00:01:07.267 --> 00:01:09.367
Welcome, Judd. I'm so happy to have you.

00:01:09.367 --> 00:01:11.434
-Ah, thanks. Very nice people.

00:01:11.434 --> 00:01:13.300
I can't see them, but they're very nice people.

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:14.868
-They sound nice.

00:01:14.868 --> 00:01:17.567
-It's not just a bunch of heads you stuck out there on sticks.

00:01:17.567 --> 00:01:19.501
That's people. -Those are real people.

00:01:19.501 --> 00:01:21.033
-Yeah. -Now, uh --

00:01:21.033 --> 00:01:23.167
-Lunch would not be a bad idea.

00:01:23.167 --> 00:01:24.534
-What's that? -Me and them.

00:01:24.534 --> 00:01:25.868
A nice little lunch.

00:01:25.868 --> 00:01:27.434
-You want to go out to lunch with the audience, now?

00:01:27.434 --> 00:01:28.767
-Why not? -Alright. There you go.

00:01:28.767 --> 00:01:31.934
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:31.934 --> 00:01:34.133
-I don't drink. -Okay.

00:01:34.133 --> 00:01:35.434
Now, Judd --

00:01:35.434 --> 00:01:36.868
-That limits everything. -Right.

00:01:36.868 --> 00:01:38.868
Now, before you offer to take everybody here out to lunch,

00:01:38.868 --> 00:01:40.701
I bet there's not a single Oscar voter here.

00:01:40.701 --> 00:01:42.567
I hope you're not trying to pander to the --

00:01:42.567 --> 00:01:44.467
-Nah. Yeah, yeah. That would be nice, wouldn't it?

00:01:44.467 --> 00:01:45.667
-Yeah. Now, this is -- -Yeah.

00:01:45.667 --> 00:01:47.033
Are you sure about that?

00:01:47.033 --> 00:01:48.267
-I'm not positive. I'm not positive about that.

00:01:48.267 --> 00:01:49.567
-Oh, okay. -This is so exciting.

00:01:49.567 --> 00:01:52.968
-No Academy members... -You might be -- If you win,

00:01:52.968 --> 00:01:55.267
you'll be the oldest Oscar winner of all time.

00:01:55.267 --> 00:01:57.000
Congratulations on the very possibility.

00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:58.334
-You had to tell 'em that.

00:01:58.334 --> 00:02:00.367
[ Laughter ]

00:02:00.367 --> 00:02:02.100
-Well, wait a minute.

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:03.534
-I wouldn't believe it. You look fantastic.

00:02:03.534 --> 00:02:06.701
-I beat Henry Fonda

00:02:06.701 --> 00:02:09.367
for the longest time between nominations.

00:02:09.367 --> 00:02:11.200
-Wow. -Right?

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:13.567
His was like 44 years and something.

00:02:13.567 --> 00:02:15.501
And mine was like 42 years and something

00:02:15.501 --> 00:02:16.934
or something like that, anyway. -Yeah.

00:02:16.934 --> 00:02:18.367
-Or maybe that was the Golden Globes.

00:02:18.367 --> 00:02:20.434
I don't know. But I beat him. -There you go.

00:02:20.434 --> 00:02:22.801
He's not walking around bragging about it right now

00:02:22.801 --> 00:02:24.167
for a lot of reasons.

00:02:24.167 --> 00:02:26.734
[ Laughter ]

00:02:26.734 --> 00:02:28.367
-Where the hell were they

00:02:28.367 --> 00:02:29.968
all those 42 years, for Christ's sake?

00:02:29.968 --> 00:02:31.501
-It has been a 42-year break.

00:02:31.501 --> 00:02:33.267
You were nominated for "Ordinary People,"

00:02:33.267 --> 00:02:34.701
a fantastic film. -Yeah.

00:02:34.701 --> 00:02:36.934
-You were nominated against your co-star, Timothy Hutton,

00:02:36.934 --> 00:02:38.467
which is surprising to me, because --

00:02:38.467 --> 00:02:39.834
-Oh, that's a terrible story.

00:02:39.834 --> 00:02:42.267
I mean, really, I was -- I mean, how --

00:02:42.267 --> 00:02:45.501
Imagine a guy gets nominated for an Academy Award

00:02:45.501 --> 00:02:47.667
and hopes he doesn't win.

00:02:47.667 --> 00:02:49.601
Because we were in the same category

00:02:49.601 --> 00:02:51.267
and he's the star of the show. -He was.

00:02:51.267 --> 00:02:53.601
-They stuck him in the same category.

00:02:53.601 --> 00:02:56.667
And -- And I thought -- I went to the ceremony

00:02:56.667 --> 00:02:59.367
and can only think of, "Oh, God, if I win this,

00:02:59.367 --> 00:03:01.467
it's going to be terrible, terrible, terrible.

00:03:01.467 --> 00:03:03.667
What am I going to do? Am I going to say,

00:03:03.667 --> 00:03:07.000
'Tim, I'm telling you, you deserved it.

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:10.901
Here. There. You deserved it.'"

00:03:10.901 --> 00:03:13.701
And I would probably go into -- I would tell the press.

00:03:13.701 --> 00:03:15.801
-Yeah. -"Sorry. "Thank you." You know?

00:03:15.801 --> 00:03:18.601
But luckily, it happened the other way around, you know?

00:03:18.601 --> 00:03:20.133
I mean, you never saw somebody

00:03:20.133 --> 00:03:21.934
who was so relieved at not winning.

00:03:21.934 --> 00:03:24.234
-So Timothy did win. -Yeah, he won.

00:03:24.234 --> 00:03:25.734
-Your co-star won. Did he offer to give you the Oscar?

00:03:25.734 --> 00:03:28.400
-No, no. No.

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:30.067
[ Laughter ]

00:03:30.067 --> 00:03:31.534
-He was 19 years old.

00:03:31.534 --> 00:03:34.734
-That's amazing. That's amazing. And an amazing film.

00:03:34.734 --> 00:03:37.267
-Yeah, yeah. We had a really good, um --

00:03:37.267 --> 00:03:40.234
Robert Redford directed the movie.

00:03:40.234 --> 00:03:43.601
It was his first time, right? By the way, he won the Tony --

00:03:43.601 --> 00:03:45.501
the Academy Award. -Yep.

00:03:45.501 --> 00:03:48.901
-And, at one time, we go through the whole movie,

00:03:48.901 --> 00:03:52.000
and it was a really, really tough movie because of the kid.

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:56.300
And he tells me the story. I'm his psychologist.

00:03:56.300 --> 00:03:59.667
And in the last scene,

00:03:59.667 --> 00:04:02.667
his girlfriend commits suicide or something like that.

00:04:02.667 --> 00:04:05.267
And they put the track in a circle.

00:04:05.267 --> 00:04:07.367
And we were going to come in and we were gonna --

00:04:07.367 --> 00:04:09.367
Redford said, "You got to hug him."

00:04:09.367 --> 00:04:11.434
This is before we started the movie.

00:04:11.434 --> 00:04:13.334
I said, "They're gonna kill you."

00:04:13.334 --> 00:04:16.067
Psychologists don't hug their client. They don't."

00:04:16.067 --> 00:04:19.000
He said, "This one will."

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:21.701
'Cause he had kids, and they never did it or something.

00:04:21.701 --> 00:04:24.267
So he puts this track around us. And we start off.

00:04:24.267 --> 00:04:26.701
And he comes in, and he's telling me the story.

00:04:26.701 --> 00:04:28.434
And I'm telling him as a psychologist,

00:04:28.434 --> 00:04:31.667
"Because. Because it wasn't you. Because you're okay.

00:04:31.667 --> 00:04:33.801
And because he died and it wasn't your fault!"

00:04:33.801 --> 00:04:37.033
And around and around and around.

00:04:37.033 --> 00:04:39.367
And we finally -- And he says,

00:04:39.367 --> 00:04:41.767
"I'm your friend. I'm your friend. Count on it."

00:04:41.767 --> 00:04:45.033
Hmm. And we did it.

00:04:45.033 --> 00:04:49.501
And Redford went, "Wow. Great. Great. Great."

00:04:49.501 --> 00:04:50.834
About a month later,

00:04:50.834 --> 00:04:52.901
I meet him on the street outside of Paramount.

00:04:52.901 --> 00:04:57.534
He's gonna shoot the scene where the kids are in the boat.

00:04:57.534 --> 00:05:00.167
I said, "Hey." He goes, "Hey."

00:05:00.167 --> 00:05:03.033
I said, "How did it come out?"

00:05:03.033 --> 00:05:04.601
He said, "I cut it."

00:05:04.601 --> 00:05:08.167
[ Laughter ]

00:05:08.167 --> 00:05:10.200
-Well, there you go. That's movie making for you.

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:12.567
-I was going use that shot for everything

00:05:12.567 --> 00:05:15.300
On every talk show I could go on. You want to see --

00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:17.200
-You should have brought it today.

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:19.300
-You want to see drama?!

00:05:19.300 --> 00:05:21.367
You want to see how well a guy can shoot a shot?

00:05:21.367 --> 00:05:23.834
-We should start -- We should start offering our guests here

00:05:23.834 --> 00:05:25.501
to bring clips from 42 years ago.

00:05:25.501 --> 00:05:27.000
-Yeah, yeah.

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:29.167
-So, "The Fabelmans." This is such a wonderful film.

00:05:29.167 --> 00:05:31.400
And you really sort of show up in this movie

00:05:31.400 --> 00:05:32.801
and you take it by storm.

00:05:32.801 --> 00:05:35.100
You yourself have lived this incredibly fascinating life.

00:05:35.100 --> 00:05:37.567
Coney Island, the Bronx, served in the Army,

00:05:37.567 --> 00:05:38.901
were an engineer for a while,

00:05:38.901 --> 00:05:42.000
and then found your way into acting.

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:43.567
-Yeah. -Are you surprised today

00:05:43.567 --> 00:05:46.234
that this thing you started out so many years ago,

00:05:46.234 --> 00:05:47.801
you're still...?

00:05:47.801 --> 00:05:49.601
-I was surprised the first day I got a job.

00:05:49.601 --> 00:05:53.334
You know, I mean, really, I was --

00:05:53.334 --> 00:05:55.334
I was everything but.

00:05:55.334 --> 00:05:57.067
I was a college guy.

00:05:57.067 --> 00:05:59.534
I mean, I was gonna be an engineer.

00:05:59.534 --> 00:06:01.801
I went through -- I got a degree in physics

00:06:01.801 --> 00:06:03.834
from the City College of New York.

00:06:03.834 --> 00:06:07.601
I then went back and became -- and for --

00:06:07.601 --> 00:06:09.767
I went to another school for art.

00:06:09.767 --> 00:06:11.601
I thought I was going to be Van Gogh.

00:06:11.601 --> 00:06:13.133
I mean -- Is that his name? -Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:06:13.133 --> 00:06:15.334
-Vincent Van Gogh?

00:06:15.334 --> 00:06:17.701
[ Laughter ] Whoa!

00:06:17.701 --> 00:06:21.567
One day out of my house, I can't remember something.

00:06:21.567 --> 00:06:23.100
And I was gonna be a painter.

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:27.601
I was gonna fly jets off the aircraft carriers.

00:06:27.601 --> 00:06:30.767
I signed up. And I went like...

00:06:30.767 --> 00:06:35.501
"You schmuck. Don't do that! You will die!"

00:06:35.501 --> 00:06:37.834
-So, I want to ask you about "Taxi."

00:06:37.834 --> 00:06:39.934
Because this is one of the greatest sitcoms of all time,

00:06:39.934 --> 00:06:41.367
and it's the first place I saw you.

00:06:41.367 --> 00:06:44.100
And I remember in the opening titles of "Taxi"

00:06:44.100 --> 00:06:46.501
your name came before the title of the show.

00:06:46.501 --> 00:06:48.200
-Whoa! -Now, at the time,

00:06:48.200 --> 00:06:50.467
as a kid watching it, and I knew I was watching reruns,

00:06:50.467 --> 00:06:52.667
I assumed, "Oh, my God. Judd Hirsch must have been

00:06:52.667 --> 00:06:55.868
a giant star for them to put his name before 'Taxi.'"

00:06:55.868 --> 00:06:58.000
-I was not. No, I was not.

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:00.167
-So were you surprised that they put your name first?

00:07:00.167 --> 00:07:04.167
-Yeah, I mean -- when they first -- Okay.

00:07:04.167 --> 00:07:06.234
I was in, like, "Rhoda."

00:07:06.234 --> 00:07:08.767
Remember that play -- that television situation?

00:07:08.767 --> 00:07:11.400
I had two episodes.

00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:14.267
And that was it. That was it.

00:07:14.267 --> 00:07:17.200
I then said, well, now I'm going to get out of television

00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:19.300
and I'm going to go to Europe and I'm going to take my kid

00:07:19.300 --> 00:07:21.801
and I'm going to do anything but.

00:07:21.801 --> 00:07:23.501
And then all of the sudden this thing comes up.

00:07:23.501 --> 00:07:26.400
And my agent said, "They want to know if you want to do this."

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:27.734
I said, "What is it?" "'Taxi.'"

00:07:27.734 --> 00:07:30.934
"Oh, is it about New York?" "Yeah, New York guy."

00:07:30.934 --> 00:07:36.400
I said, "Well, uh, um... Alright. I'll read it."

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:38.033
-Could your agent read from your face

00:07:38.033 --> 00:07:40.033
you weren't that excited about it?

00:07:40.033 --> 00:07:43.367
-Nah. They're never with you.

00:07:43.367 --> 00:07:46.734
"You want it? You want to do it? Okay.

00:07:46.734 --> 00:07:48.400
What should I tell them?"

00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:50.200
I said -- I said -- This is actual.

00:07:50.200 --> 00:07:52.400
I said, "Make them an offer they can't accept."

00:07:52.400 --> 00:07:55.667
[ Laughter ]

00:07:55.667 --> 00:07:57.367
Don't do this if you're an actor.

00:07:57.367 --> 00:08:00.400
Don't do this. Because they're liable not to.

00:08:00.400 --> 00:08:03.300
So he says, "Okay, they accept it."

00:08:03.300 --> 00:08:05.767
I said, "Why?

00:08:05.767 --> 00:08:09.234
It's going to be at least three years, and I'm 40."

00:08:09.234 --> 00:08:11.167
-Yeah. -You know?

00:08:11.167 --> 00:08:13.033
Shh. I was 42. -They had no idea you'd be

00:08:13.033 --> 00:08:15.267
getting Oscar nominations well into your 80s.

00:08:15.267 --> 00:08:16.567
-No. -Yeah.

00:08:16.567 --> 00:08:19.300
-I mean, if they did, the name would have been larger.

00:08:19.300 --> 00:08:21.601
[ Light laughter ]

00:08:21.601 --> 00:08:22.834
-Okay, okay, okay. So what's left?

00:08:22.834 --> 00:08:24.234
They said, "Billing."

00:08:24.234 --> 00:08:26.868
And I said, "Oh, well, just -- What do they usually do?"

00:08:26.868 --> 00:08:31.801
"Taxi" starring Judd Hirsch, Danny DeVito, ba ba ba ba ba.

00:08:31.801 --> 00:08:34.601
However. However. It doesn't make any difference to me.

00:08:34.601 --> 00:08:39.367
Okay. Opening night, we go to the producer's house.

00:08:39.367 --> 00:08:41.567
And the whole cast is there at a bar in his house.

00:08:41.567 --> 00:08:44.801
And he's got a TV up there. And I'm standing way behind.

00:08:44.801 --> 00:08:47.033
And we never saw the show.

00:08:47.033 --> 00:08:50.167
And we hear -- [ Imitating "Taxi" theme song ]

00:08:50.167 --> 00:08:53.234
The music. The cab. Across the bridge.

00:08:53.234 --> 00:08:55.501
The word "Judd Hirsch."

00:08:55.501 --> 00:08:59.000
[ Laughter ]

00:08:59.000 --> 00:09:03.300
They changed the name of the show?!

00:09:03.300 --> 00:09:04.734
"Taxi."

00:09:04.734 --> 00:09:06.934
So I go, "Oh, my God."

00:09:06.934 --> 00:09:09.501
When they talked about billing, I never dreamed.

00:09:09.501 --> 00:09:11.100
There's no other show I've ever seen.

00:09:11.100 --> 00:09:12.801
-Ever seen. How did the rest of the cast

00:09:12.801 --> 00:09:14.267
feel that your name came before "Taxi"?

00:09:14.267 --> 00:09:17.133
-I luckily never asked them. -Okay. Good.

00:09:17.133 --> 00:09:18.501
That's good advice.

00:09:18.501 --> 00:09:19.767
-To this day, I still don't know.

00:09:19.767 --> 00:09:20.868
-Well, that's very good.

00:09:20.868 --> 00:09:22.133
-If you ask them, they'll be very polite.

00:09:22.133 --> 00:09:25.100
"Oh, yes, of course! He was the big star!

00:09:25.100 --> 00:09:28.267
He deserved --" Bull [bleep].

00:09:28.267 --> 00:09:31.501
If I was one of them, I wouldn't think that way.

00:09:31.501 --> 00:09:33.234
-Well, what a delight to have you here.

00:09:33.234 --> 00:09:35.634
Congratulations on your nomination. Well-deserved.

00:09:35.634 --> 00:09:38.334
[ Cheers and applause ] -Judd Hirsch, everybody.

00:09:38.334 --> 00:09:39.734
-Thank you.

00:09:39.734 --> 00:09:41.601
-"The Fabelmans" is in theaters and on demand.

00:09:41.601 --> 00:09:43.300
We'll be right back with Justice Smith.

